  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 325 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A Heat Advisory remains in effect today from 11 AM \nto 8 PM for all but the far wrn/srn Piedmont. Another will likely be \nneeded for Fri. \n\nA lower mid-latitude/sub-tropical ridge centered over the srn \nAppalachians, observed at 595 dam and 324 dam in the 500-700 mb \nlayer on Wed, will persist in a favorable location for excessive \nheat in cntl NC for the next couple of days. \n\nPersistence surface temperatures, to 1-2 F hotter ones especially \nover the nrn Piedmont, will likely result today and combine with \nmixed/afternoon dewpoints in the upr 60s to lwr 70s. Related heat \nindex values will range between 100-105 F over the nw Piedmont to \nmostly 105-108 F in the Advisory area. Any convection and outflow \nwill likely not develop until late afternoon-evening, with limited \nrelief from the excessive heat.  \n\nMean mixing ratios and surface dewpoints are forecast to increase by \nFri, when mixed/afternoon dewpoints are likely to be in the lwr 70s. \nThat uptick in dewpoints will likely yield the highest heat index \nvalues of this stretch on Fri, when values near 110 F will be \npossible at the most reliable observation sites from the nrn \nSandhills to the ne Piedmont and Coastal Plain (ie. not IXA and CTZ, \nwhere values around 115 F will probably result from \nunrepresentative/high-biased surface dewpoints at those sites). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered storms will focus across the far nrn/ne \nPiedmont and nrn Coastal Plain the next couple of afternoons-\nevenings, with an accompanying risk of damaging, multi-cell \ndownbursts. \n\nNwly flow aloft, around the aforementioned ridge centered over the \nsrn Appalachians, will gradually strengthen and be accompanied by \nweakly falling heights and cooling through the mid-levels over the \nsrn Middle Atlantic by Fri-Fri night, as a cyclone progresses sewd \nacross srn QC. \n\nMeanwhile at the surface, a lee/pre-frontal trough will remain over \nVA/NC, preceding both a synoptic cold front and composite outflow \nthat will gradually settle swd from the lwr Great Lakes and nrn \nMiddle Atlantic this morning. While both will probably remain to the \nnorth of cntl NC until Sat, other aggregate outflows will probably \ndevelop swd across VA and into the nrn Piedmont and nrn Coastal \nPlain of NC later this afternoon-evening and more-so Fri afternoon-\nevening, the latter when overall convective coverage over the srn \nMiddle Atlantic will probably be greater of the two days. While the \nstrongest nwly flow will remain across the nrn Middle Atlantic \nthrough the period, where relatively greatest severe potential will \nexist, continued high DCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg will support a risk of \ndamaging downbursts from pulse/multicells today. The thermodynamic \nenvironment will be similar on Fri, with a slightly greater \nsevere/wind risk from multicell clusters organized by an increase in \nnwly, mid-level flow to 15-25 kts over nrn and nern NC by late Fri-\nFri night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... A cold front will pass through the region Saturday \nnight into Sunday, bringing the best chance of rain on Saturday \nfollowed by a cool down closer to normal.\n\nSaturday should be the last hot day of this stretch, with high \ntemperatures in the 90s and heat indices generally expected in the \nmid 90s to mid 100s in the warm sector ahead of an approaching cold \nfront. This should provide plenty of instability for storm \nformation, with models showing around 1000-2000 J/kg of CAPE \nSaturday afternoon. There also looks to be around 25-35 kts of \nshear, to allow for storms to sustain and create a potential severe \nstorm threat. Depending on the timing of the rain, the main threat \nshould be strong to severe winds and water-loaded downbursts within \nclusters of storms Saturday afternoon into the evening. Most model \nguidance is suggesting up to an inch of rain may be possible on \nSaturday, with isolated higher amounts possible. Some guidance is \nstill suggesting that some rain may linger into Sunday depending on \nthe timing of the front, however the chance of this seems to be \ndecreasing. \n\nThe cold front should move through the region sometime Saturday \nnight into Sunday morning which will usher in cooler and drier air \nto the region as high pressure builds in. High temperatures should \nreturn to the 80s from Sunday through Tuesday, before potentially \nreturning to the 90s midweek. The chance of rain during this period \nwill be generally low each day.",
